<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Romanov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1613">1613</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Romanov dynasty, Romanov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Romanov was the dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> as tsar in 1613 until the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> in 1917. Descended from a Muscovite boyar family related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> through his first wife Anastasia, it produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, and from 1762 the throne passed to its Holstein-Gottorp-Romanov branch. Nicholas II and his family were killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov.jpg" alt="House of Romanov" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taubiy (original uploader) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_Romanoff.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Romanovs share their origin with about two dozen other Russian noble families. Their earliest common ancestor is Andrei Kobyla, attested about 1347 as a boyar in the service of Simeon, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Later genealogies gave him a distinguished pedigree, including descent from an Old Prussian prince, a legend contested by another version that derives the family from a boyar line of Novgorod. His descendants took the names Koshkin, Zakharin and Zakharin-Yuriev, and the grandchildren of Roman Yurievich Zakharyin-Yuriev took the name Romanov.</p><p>The family rose when Roman's daughter Anastasia Zakharyina married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> on 3 (13) February 1547 and was crowned the first tsaritsa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>'s party prevailed in 1598, he accused Feodor Nikitich Romanov of treason in 1600 and had the family deported to remote parts of the north and the Urals, where most died; Feodor was forced to become a monk with the name Filaret.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a> ended with Feodor I in 1598, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> followed. On 21 February 1613 the Zemsky Sobor elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> as tsar. His son Alexei succeeded him in 1645, and after Alexei's death in 1676 his children by two marriages struggled for the throne.</p><p>Peter I, who ruled from 1682 to 1725, proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1721 and, through wars and reforms, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> a major European power. The direct male line ended when Elizabeth died childless in 1762. Her nephew Peter III, of the House of Holstein-Gottorp, succeeded and kept his mother's house name, so later monarchs are sometimes called Holstein-Gottorp-Romanov. His wife overthrew him and reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>.</p><p>Her son Paul I issued the Pauline Laws, which set semi-Salic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> and required the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> faith. He was murdered in 1801. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> came Nicholas I in 1825, whose accession met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>, who freed the serfs in 1861. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>'s abdication on 15 March [O.S. 2 March] 1917, ending 304 years of Romanov rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the abdication <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and his family were held at the Alexander Palace, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a> in August 1917 and moved to Yekaterinburg in April 1918. On the night of 16 to 18 July 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> executed the entire family and several retainers. Of the house's 65 members, 47 survivors went into exile. In 1924 Grand Duke Kirill Vladimirovich claimed the headship of the defunct imperial house. Living descendants of other members of the imperial house remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Romanov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Russian Empire collapsed in February 1917 when long-term weaknesses met the pressures of the First World War, in a political crisis that the <b>Romanov</b> autocracy proved institutionally incapable of managing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Romanov</b> tsars Michael (r. 1613–1645), Alexei (r. 1645–1676) and Feodor III (r. 1676–1682) strengthened the bureaucracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the palace revolutionaries of 1725–1825, who wanted to put their own candidate on the throne, but unlike earlier <b>Romanov</b> palace revolutions their rising has been considered the beginning of a revolutionary movement: it was the first open breach between the government and the reformist nobility, which would widen.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>An assembly of the land chose sixteen-year-old Mikhail <b>Romanov</b> as tsar (21 February Old Style), ending the Time of Troubles.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Over three centuries of <b>Romanov</b> rule ended, and a Provisional Government took power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-15">15 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
